AI : chatGpt, Claude, Deepseek, Meta AI, Kimi 

Abstract

This document presents the completed theoretical and technical specification of the NIMP framework. It unifies the programme’s philosophical architecture, hypothesis‑extraction formalism, measurement models, and empirical pipeline into a single, internally consistent instrument. The hard core is a substantive psychological claim, abandonable if exile‑derived constructs reveal no structural difference from terrestrial analogues. A single Bayesian posterior‑probability rule governs all empirical decisions. Hypothesis extraction is governed by a dual‑metric reliability protocol and a fully auditable coding trail. The primary empirical package is built around five hypotheses extracted from the Goldorak narrative, advanced to a pre‑registered VR experiment and meta‑analytic integration with analog data. All measurement thresholds conform to current psychometric standards. The Progressivity Ratio is retained as a novel methodological contribution. This specification constitutes the analytical backbone of the doctoral thesis.

---

1. Positioning and Formal Architecture

NIMP is a formalisation of Science Fiction Prototyping (Johnson, 2011) and Design Fiction (Sterling, 2005). Its generative core is the mapping

\Phi: \mathcal{N} \to \mathcal{H},

taking a narrative corpus \mathcal{N} to a set of formal psychological hypotheses \mathcal{H}. Each h \in \mathcal{H} is a tuple

h = (X, Y, \delta, \text{source\_segment}),

where X is a predictor construct, Y an outcome construct, \delta \in \{-1, 0, +1\} a signed directional claim, and \text{source\_segment} a pointer back to the textual evidence. The retained source‑segment pointer makes \Phi an auditable function: a sceptical reader can retrieve the passage and evaluate the inference directly, rather than trusting the coders’ agreement alone. This is the property that most clearly distinguishes NIMP from informal narrative‑as‑commentary approaches.

\Phi is not unconstrained pattern‑matching. It operates over a fixed ontology of psychological constructs pre‑anchored to documented gaps in space behavioural health (cognitive, affective, behavioural, social, and mental‑health domains; ESA white paper, NASA BHP standard measures). The content of each hypothesis arises from the text; the category system into which it is expressed comes from the field. This constraint tightens the mapping’s domain without weakening its bottom‑up character.

2. Hypothesis Extraction from Narrative

2.1 Coding Reliability – A Dual‑Metric Standard

A single reliability statistic is insufficient, because each available metric is blind to a different failure mode. The protocol therefore requires:

· Cohen’s \kappa, with the threshold \kappa \geq 0.80 (excellent agreement).
· Gwet’s AC1, as a robustness check when tag prevalence is skewed (certain constructs will appear far more often than others across a large corpus).
· Cosine semantic similarity between the vector embeddings of each coder’s tag set, with an equivalence threshold > 0.70. This metric captures near‑miss disagreements (e.g., “grief” vs. “solastalgia‑adjacent distress”) that register as plain disagreement under \kappa and AC1.

All three metrics are reported together in every reliability table; they are not redundant checks but complementary closures of specific interpretive gaps.

At least 20% of the corpus is double‑coded by two independent coders. Disagreements are reconciled through discussion, and the final agreed tag set per segment, T(s) = c_1(s) \cap c_2(s) after reconciliation, forms the input to hypothesis generation.

2.2 Hypothesis Generation Algorithm

1. Construct Identification. Tags are mapped to psychological constructs \psi drawn from the pre‑defined ontology.
2. Hypothesis Formulation. For each segment s, all co‑occurring construct pairs (\psi_i, \psi_j) are considered. A directional hypothesis h_{ij} is generated when the narrative resolution implies a causal or predictive link \psi_i \Rightarrow \psi_j. The direction is coded as +1, -1, or 0 based on explicit narrative statements. The codebook provides worked examples of directional mapping to stabilise inter‑rater decisions. If a moderator M is narratively present, a moderation hypothesis h_{ijk} is produced: (\psi_i \times M) \Rightarrow \psi_j.
3. Negative Case Inclusion. A dedicated set of “failure‑mode” tags ({rigidity, isolation, guilt, vengeance, technological hubris}) ensures systematic coding of adverse psychological dynamics when they are present. No artificial quota is imposed; the codebook simply guarantees that negative content, if in the narrative, is captured.
4. Hypothesis Pruning. Only hypotheses supported by at least two distinct segments or one segment explicitly flagged as a “core theme” by both coders are retained. This salience threshold limits the hypothesis space to claims with non‑trivial narrative weight.
5. Falsifiability Encoding. Each retained hypothesis is paired with a test statistic, a null model, and the unified Bayesian decision rule defined in §5. The number of hypotheses advanced to empirical test is capped at ten (five primary) to maintain statistical power and multiple‑comparisons control.

3. Application to the Primary Corpus: Goldorak

3.1 Corpus Definition

The primary corpus is the original 74‑episode Japanese series UFO Robo Grendizer (Toei Animation, 1975–1977), coded from the Japanese‑language scripts where available and from a verified English translation otherwise. The French Goldorak adaptation, which materially alters dialogue register and characterisation emphasis in places, is retained only as secondary comparative material and is never pooled with the primary‑corpus coding for reliability or hypothesis‑extraction purposes.

3.2 Codebook and Procedure

The codebook operationalises each construct in the fixed ontology with explicit inclusion/exclusion criteria and worked examples. It is developed iteratively against a pilot subset of episodes until all three reliability metrics exceed their thresholds on the pilot set. In the full double‑coding phase, reconciled tags form T(s) for each segment.

3.3 Extracted Hypothesis Set

Applying the extraction algorithm to the full corpus, and pruning to the pre‑registered cap, yields the following five primary hypotheses, each traceable to specific narrative evidence:

