These five hypotheses are the empirical payload of the framework. Chapters 8.4–8.5 of the thesis will test them; the remainder of this specification describes the instruments and inferential machinery that make those tests possible.

4. Mathematical Modeling of Psychological Constructs

4.1 The Astro‑Exile Grief Inventory (AEGI)

The AEGI captures the traumatic bereavement and place‑identity loss following permanent, irrevocable separation from Earth. It is closer to traumatic grief than to solastalgia (which concerns distress in an inhabited but altered environment). The initial item pool adapts items from validated grief and place‑attachment scales, replacing references to environmental change with the permanent loss of Earth (e.g., “I feel a deep sense of grief when I imagine never seeing Earth’s forests again”).

Measurement model (Confirmatory Factor Analysis):

y_i = \lambda_i \eta + \varepsilon_i, \quad \varepsilon_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_i^2),

with latent factor \eta (Astro‑Exile Grief). Model fit targets:

\chi^2/df < 3,\quad CFI > 0.95,\quad RMSEA < 0.06,\quad SRMR < 0.08 .

Composite reliability must satisfy \omega_h = (\sum \lambda_i)^2 / \big((\sum \lambda_i)^2 + \sum \sigma_i^2\big) > 0.80, ideally > 0.90. A 5‑item short form will be extracted for analog mission use, with \alpha > 0.7.

4.2 Cross‑Cultural Measurement Invariance

Invariance testing will be conducted across at least two distinct cultural groups (Western individualist and MENA collectivist). The sequence configural \to metric \to scalar uses \Delta CFI < 0.01 and \Delta RMSEA \leq 0.015 as criteria. If scalar invariance fails, alignment‑based approximate invariance methods are invoked as a pre‑registered fallback.

4.3 Physiological and Analog Measurement

For analog missions (n \approx 12{-}16), single‑indicator composite scores or the AEGI short form will be used. The physiological link model is

\eta_{\text{phys}} = \beta_0 + \beta_1 \eta_{\text{psych}} + \epsilon, \quad \epsilon \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_\epsilon^2),

with no latent variable regression attempted. The expected correlation between cognitive reappraisal and vagally‑mediated HRV is r \approx 0.3 (based on meta‑analytic evidence).

5. Falsifiability, Evidence Integration, and Programme Structure

5.1 Unified Bayesian Falsification Rule

Every hypothesis h is adjudicated by the same single rule:

h \text{ is falsified if } P(\theta > 0.1 \mid D_{\text{all}}) < 0.1,

h \text{ is corroborated if } P(\theta > 0.1 \mid D_{\text{all}}) > 0.95.

\theta is expressed as a standardised mean difference, correlation, or standardised regression coefficient \beta^* for interactions. D_{\text{all}} pools the primary laboratory VR evidence with the secondary analog meta‑analysis (§5.2). Frequentist confidence intervals and the Bayes factor BF_{01} are reported as convergent evidence, not as independent decision boundaries.

5.2 Bayesian Evidence Integration

Posterior distributions are obtained via a Bayesian random‑effects meta‑analysis:

\theta_j \sim \mathcal{N}(\mu, \tau^2), \quad \hat{\theta}_j \sim \mathcal{N}(\theta_j, \sigma_j^2),

with priors \mu \sim \mathcal{N}(0,1) and \tau \sim \text{Half‑Cauchy}(0, 0.5). The between‑study variance \tau^2 is explicitly reported; a large \tau^2 indicates that lab‑ and analog‑elicited versions of a construct differ, and this is treated as a finding rather than a nuisance.

5.3 Lakatosian Programme Architecture

Hard Core:
Psychological constructs derived from narratives of permanent planetary exile reflect real‑world mechanisms (grief, identity reconfiguration, resilience) that share nomological networks with terrestrial constructs but possess unique structural features due to total, irrevocable loss of a home planet.

This claim is abandonable. The programme is judged to have failed if, after the full empirical package, no exile‑specific structural difference from existing grief, displacement, or isolation constructs can be demonstrated. Everything else—the extraction operator, the reliability metrics, the measurement models—belongs to the protective belt and is revisable.

Protective Belt and Ad Hoc Modifications:
A belt adjustment in response to a failed prediction is permitted only when it generates at least one novel, pre‑registered prediction not contained in the original belt. Any change not meeting this condition is classified as an ad hoc modification.

Progressivity Ratio (PR):

PR = \frac{\text{corroborated novel predictions}}{\text{ad hoc modifications}} .

A PR < 1 over the series of studies signals a degenerating programme and prompts abandonment of the relevant auxiliary. The metric’s behaviour at small hypothesis counts will be characterised via Monte Carlo simulation as part of the thesis’s methodological contribution; its numerical instability at low counts is reported as a limitation of the metric itself.

6. Empirical Pipeline

6.1 Primary VR Earth‑Out‑of‑View Experiment

Participants (n \geq 200) are immersed in a virtual Martian habitat with windows showing only starfield (Earth absent). Pre‑ and post‑immersion measures include the AEGI, the Cultural Ritual Frequency log, the Post‑Traumatic Growth Inventory, and relevant resilience scales. The primary analysis tests the NIMP‑derived hypotheses, with the ritual buffering interaction as the headline model:

\text{AEGI}_{\text{post}} = \beta_0 + \beta_1 \text{Ritual} + \beta_2 \text{AEGI}_{\text{pre}} + \beta_3 \text{Group} + \beta_4 (\text{Ritual} \times \text{Group}) + \epsilon.

Group contrasts Western vs. MENA cultural background. Multiple comparisons are controlled via Benjamini‑Hochberg correction. Power: the planned sample provides 80% power to detect a medium interaction (f^2 = 0.10); a sequential Bayes factor design with interim analysis at n=120 is pre‑registered to allow early stopping for efficacy or futility.

6.2 Meta‑Analytic Integration

Published effect sizes from analog missions (HERA, SIRIUS, Concordia) are extracted and integrated via the hierarchical model in §5.2. Because raw participant‑level data are unlikely to be accessible, aggregated effect sizes and sampling variances will be used. The posterior of the overall effect \mu is then evaluated under the unified falsification rule.

6.3 Secondary Narratives and NLP Extension

The exploratory coding of Aniara and an Indigenous futurism text will be conducted under collaborative, community‑consent protocols and reported as a reflexivity chapter, not as a core deliverable. The NLP‑assisted hypothesis‑mining prototype, using the all-MiniLM-L6-v2 embedding model and the scoring function S(h) = \alpha \cdot \text{relevance} + \beta \cdot \text{novelty} - \gamma \cdot \text{overlap}, will be presented as a proof‑of‑concept for postdoctoral work.

7. Technical Milestones (Thesis Deliverables)

1. Formal NIMP framework with a unified Bayesian falsification rule, dual‑metric reliability protocol, and transparent Lakatosian architecture.
2. Auditable hypothesis extraction from Goldorak, yielding five pre‑registered, testable hypotheses with full coding trail and reliability statistics exceeding all thresholds.
3. Validated Astro‑Exile Grief Inventory (AEGI), with factorial validity, \omega_h > 0.80, and cross‑cultural measurement invariance evidence.
4. One fully pre‑registered VR laboratory study (n \geq 200, sequential Bayes factor design) testing the five NIMP‑derived hypotheses.
5. Bayesian meta‑analytic integration of published analog mission data, demonstrating the evidence‑updating logic.
6. Progressivity Ratio as an original methodological contribution, with Monte Carlo sensitivity analysis.
7. NLP prototype and exploratory secondary case analyses as groundwork for postdoctoral research.

By incorporating the refined formal architecture and the concrete Goldorak extraction, this specification transforms narrative imagination into a fully operational, falsifiable scientific instrument for the psychology of multiplanetary existence. The hard core is substantive; the empirical test is precisely defined; and the failure condition is stated plainly enough that the programme can, in principle, be proved wrong.

