Appendix A. Formal Architecture of the Extraction Operator

A.1 Definition of the Mapping

The extraction operator is

\Phi : \mathcal{N} \to \mathcal{H},

where \mathcal{N} is a narrative corpus (a set of segments s \in S drawn from one or more texts) and \mathcal{H} is a set of formal hypotheses. Each hypothesis h \in \mathcal{H} is a tuple

h = (X, Y, \delta, \sigma),

with
X, Y \in \Psi, the fixed ontology of psychological constructs;
\delta \in \{-1,0,+1\} the directional claim;
\sigma \subseteq S the set of source segments that jointly support the hypothesis (the “source pointer”).

A.2 Auditability Property

Proposition (Auditability). For every h \in \mathcal{H} produced by \Phi, the source set \sigma is non‑empty and publicly retrievable. Therefore, any independent researcher can inspect the narrative evidence that gave rise to h without recourse to the original coders.

Proof. The hypothesis generation algorithm (§2.2) only creates h if either (a) at least two distinct segments contain the co‑occurring construct pair, or (b) one segment is flagged as a core theme by both coders. In either case, the segment identifiers are recorded in \sigma during extraction. The narrative corpus \mathcal{N} is publicly available (the Japanese scripts and a verified translation are accessible). Hence \sigma is non‑empty and the textual evidence is retrievable. ∎

A.3 Replicability and the Dual‑Metric Standard

Replicability of \Phi means that two independent teams applying the same codebook to the same corpus will produce hypothesis sets whose content agrees beyond chance. Because no single agreement statistic captures all failure modes, we employ a triple of metrics (cf. Appendix B). The operator is considered replicable when all three metrics exceed their pre‑registered thresholds on the double‑coded sample.

