Appendix B. Reliability Metrics: Formulas and Justification

B.1 Cohen’s Kappa

Let p_o be the proportion of observed agreement and p_e the proportion expected by chance. Then

\kappa = \frac{p_o - p_e}{1 - p_e}.

We require \kappa \ge 0.80, corresponding to “excellent” agreement (Landis & Koch, 1977). This threshold ensures that the assignment of discrete tags is far beyond random concordance.

B.2 Gwet’s AC1

When the prevalence of some tags is very low (e.g., “technological hubris” appears in <2% of segments), \kappa can be paradoxically low even when agreement is high. Gwet’s AC1 corrects for this by modelling chance agreement differently:

AC1 = \frac{p_a - p_{e}^{\gamma}}{1 - p_{e}^{\gamma}},

where p_a is the observed agreement and p_{e}^{\gamma} is a chance‑agreement probability that does not depend on the marginal distributions in the same way as \kappa’s p_e. The AC1 is reported alongside \kappa for every reliability analysis; if \kappa drops below 0.80 but AC1 remains above 0.80, the disagreement is attributed to prevalence imbalance rather than coding inconsistency.

B.3 Cosine Semantic Similarity

Each coder’s assignment for segment s is a set of tags T_1(s), T_2(s). These tags are embedded in a 384‑dimensional space using the all-MiniLM-L6-v2 model. The segment‑level similarity is the cosine of the mean tag vectors:

\text{sim}\big(T_1(s), T_2(s)\big) = \frac{\bar{\mathbf{v}}_1 \cdot \bar{\mathbf{v}}_2}{\|\bar{\mathbf{v}}_1\| \, \|\bar{\mathbf{v}}_2\|},

with \bar{\mathbf{v}}_i = \frac{1}{|T_i|} \sum_{t \in T_i} \mathbf{v}_t. The corpus‑level cosine similarity is the mean over all double‑coded segments. We set the equivalence threshold at > 0.70, following recent validation work on multi‑LLM thematic analysis (arXiv framework, 2024). This metric distinguishes genuine interpretive conflict from terminological drift (e.g., “grief” vs. “solastalgia‑adjacent distress”); a near‑miss that \kappa counts as a full disagreement will register as high cosine similarity, alerting the team to a codebook refinement opportunity rather than a reliability failure.

B.4 Joint Decision Rule

The extraction is accepted as reliable if and only if all of the following hold on the double‑coded sample:

· \kappa \ge 0.80
· AC1 \ge 0.80
· Mean cosine similarity > 0.70

If any metric falls below its threshold, iterative codebook revision and reconciliation continue until all three are met.

