Appendix F. Bayesian Evidence Integration and Falsification Rule

F.1 Hierarchical Model for Multi‑Source Evidence

The evidence from k studies (laboratory + analog mission effect sizes) is combined as:

\hat{\theta}_j \sim \mathcal{N}(\theta_j, \sigma_j^2), \quad \theta_j \sim \mathcal{N}(\mu, \tau^2),

\mu \sim \mathcal{N}(0, 1), \quad \tau \sim \text{Half‑Cauchy}(0, 0.5).

The overall effect \mu is the parameter of interest. The posterior p(\mu \mid \mathbf{D}) is obtained via MCMC. The unified falsification rule is evaluated on \mu:

· Falsified if P(\mu > 0.1 \mid \mathbf{D}) < 0.1
· Corroborated if P(\mu > 0.1 \mid \mathbf{D}) > 0.95

The negligible threshold 0.1 corresponds to a standardised mean difference that is “trivially small” (a ROPE, Region of Practical Equivalence, from -\infty to 0.1). The decision boundary 0.1 on the posterior probability is a conventional strict threshold that controls the false‑corroboration rate.

F.2 Prior Sensitivity Analysis

The primary analysis uses a Cauchy prior with scale 0.5 on the study‑level effects. We will repeat all inferences under a range of scale parameters (0.1, 0.5, 1.0) and report the maximum change in the posterior probability of the effect exceeding 0.1. If the conclusion (falsified / corroborated / indeterminate) changes across prior scales, the result is flagged as prior‑sensitive.

