Appendix G. The Progressivity Ratio: Definition and Monte Carlo Evaluation

G.1 Formal Definition

Let P_{\text{novel}} be the set of pre‑registered novel predictions derived from the programme’s hard core and protective belt, and let M_{\text{ad hoc}} be the number of modifications to the protective belt that were not pre‑registered and were introduced solely to rescue a failed prediction. Then

PR = \frac{|P_{\text{novel}} \text{ corroborated}|}{|M_{\text{ad hoc}}|},

where “corroborated” means the prediction survived the unified falsification rule. A PR < 1 indicates that ad hoc adjustments outnumber corroborated novel facts, the hallmark of a degenerating research programme (Lakatos, 1970).

G.2 Monte Carlo Simulation of PR Stability

Because the thesis generates a small number of hypotheses (maximum 10 tested), the PR can be highly sensitive to a single corroboration or modification. We conduct a simulation study:

· Assume a “true” programme with a fixed probability p that a novel prediction is true and will be corroborated.
· Simulate 10,000 runs of a sequence of 5–10 tests, each with a randomly generated modification probability q (the chance that a failed prediction triggers an ad hoc modification).
· Compute the empirical distribution of PR under various (p,q) scenarios.

Results (to be reported in Chapter 8.5) will show the range of PR values that can arise by chance when the programme is actually progressive (p high, q low). The instability of PR at small counts will be acknowledged as a limitation of the metric, and the thesis will recommend that PR be used as a qualitative heuristic rather than a strict decision rule until the hypothesis corpus is substantially larger.

