Appendix I. NLP‑Assisted Hypothesis Mining: Technical Specification

I.1 Embedding Model and Similarity

All narrative segments and construct definitions are encoded using all-MiniLM-L6-v2, which maps text to a 384‑dimensional unit sphere. The semantic similarity between two text fragments A, B is

\text{sim}(A, B) = \frac{\mathbf{v}_A \cdot \mathbf{v}_B}{\|\mathbf{v}_A\| \|\mathbf{v}_B\|}.

I.2 Hypothesis Scoring Function

The system scores candidate hypotheses extracted by an LLM prompted with the codebook. The score is

S(h) = \alpha \cdot \text{rel}(h) + \beta \cdot \text{nov}(h) - \gamma \cdot \text{ov}(h),

where
\text{rel}(h) = \text{sim}(h_{\text{text}}, \text{corpus}) (relevance to narrative),
\text{nov}(h) = 1 - \max_{c \in \mathcal{C}} \text{sim}(h, c) (novelty relative to existing space psychology constructs \mathcal{C}),
\text{ov}(h) = \text{mean cosine similarity with already‑extracted hypotheses} (redundancy).

Weights (\alpha, \beta, \gamma) are tuned on the manually extracted Goldorak hypothesis set to maximise rank correlation with the coders’ salience ratings. The tuned system is then applied to secondary narratives as a proof‑of‑concept; its output is not used in the primary confirmatory tests.

I.3 Validation

The LLM’s inter‑rater reliability with the human coders is computed on the same double‑coded sample using the triple‑metric standard (κ, AC1, cosine). Only if it matches or exceeds human‑human reliability is the pipeline considered a viable assistant for future large‑scale work.

